import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Equation from "../../components/Equation.astro";
---
<BaseLayout
  title="What the FFT Reveals About Gradient Exclusion — AI Research"
  description="A Fourier-domain interpretation of gradient exclusion in spatially adaptive reflection refinement, and why it does not directly define an FFT-based tile router."
>
  <article>
    <div class="meta">Computer Vision · Fourier Analysis · Optimization</div>
    <h1>What the FFT Reveals About Gradient Exclusion</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      <em>Can the frequency domain tell us where an image needs refinement?</em>
    </p>

    <p>
      Suppose you are trying to separate a photograph taken through glass into
      two layers: the scene behind the glass and the reflection on its surface.
      You have an initial estimate of the scene, a residual that captures what
      the estimate has not explained, and a limited budget for improving the
      result. You cannot refine every region equally. Somewhere in the image,
      your next update needs to do more good than harm.
    </p>

    <p>
      The question is where to spend it. In the
      <a href="https://github.com/shubhojit/adaptive-reflection-refinement" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">adaptive reflection-refinement prototype</a>,
      a router scores image tiles using the overlap between the gradients of the
      estimated transmission and its residual. Tiles with stronger overlap
      receive priority for refinement.
    </p>

    <p>
      It is a spatial heuristic. But gradients have another interpretation:
      they are operators whose responses depend on spatial frequency. That
      suggests a tempting possibility. <strong>If gradient exclusion is
      sensitive to spatial scale, could its Fourier representation tell us
      which regions deserve refinement?</strong>
    </p>

    <p>
      To answer that, we need to distinguish three things that are easy to
      conflate: the frequency response of a finite difference, the actual
      objective implemented by the code, and the information required to make a
      useful routing decision. The connection between them is more subtle than it
      first appears.
    </p>

    <h2>First, what are we trying to separate?</h2>
    <p>
      Let <strong>I</strong> be the observed image, <strong>T</strong> the
      clean transmission layer we want, and <strong>R</strong> the reflection
      layer. A simple mixture model is:
    </p>
    <Equation
      expression={String.raw`I = (1-\alpha)T + \alpha R`}
      label="Observed image I is a mixture of transmission T and reflection R with mixing strength alpha"
    />
    <p class="math-translation">
      <strong>Translating the math:</strong> Each observed pixel mixes the
      scene behind the glass with a reflected scene. Given only the mixture,
      many different pairs of layers could explain the same image.
    </p>
    <p>
      That ambiguity matters. The prototype's gradient-exclusion term is a
      prior: it favors decompositions in which transmission and residual
      gradients do not strongly coincide. It is a useful modeling choice, not a
      physical law that tells us exactly which layer produced each edge.
    </p>

    <h2>What does a gradient actually measure?</h2>
    <p>
      Imagine three images. One contains a broad, slowly varying patch of
      brightness. Another contains a series of sharp edges. The third alternates
      between bright and dark pixels. A finite difference responds differently to
      each. Broad variations tend to produce small differences; rapid changes
      produce larger ones. But the response is not unlimited: on a discrete pixel
      grid, the exact response is shaped by the sampling structure itself.
    </p>
    <p>
      Under the periodic-boundary convention used by the discrete Fourier
      transform, consider a one-dimensional signal <strong>x[n]</strong>. Its forward
      difference is <strong>x[n+1] − x[n]</strong>. The FFT makes that intuition precise:
      a shift becomes multiplication by a complex phase.
    </p>
    <Equation
      expression={String.raw`\widehat{D_x x}(\omega_x) = \left(e^{i\omega_x}-1\right)\hat{x}(\omega_x), \qquad \left|e^{i\omega_x}-1\right|^2 = 4\sin^2\!\left(\frac{\omega_x}{2}\right)`}
      label="The Fourier transform of a forward finite difference multiplies each frequency by e to the i omega minus one, with squared magnitude four sine squared of omega over two"
    />
    <p class="math-translation">
      <strong>Translating the math:</strong> A finite difference suppresses very
      slow variation and responds more strongly as spatial frequency rises. On a
      pixel grid, the exact gain is the sine-shaped factor shown here—not simply
      frequency squared at every scale.
    </p>
    <p>
      The useful mental model is that a gradient is not merely a detector of
      edges. It is a scale-sensitive operator, and its response is determined by
      the spatial frequencies present in the signal. In two dimensions, horizontal
      and vertical differences have their own frequency responses. Together they
      give a frequency weight <strong>4 sin²(ω<sub>x</sub>/2) + 4 sin²(ω<sub>y</sub>/2)</strong>.
      At low frequencies this behaves approximately like
      <strong>ω<sub>x</sub>² + ω<sub>y</sub>²</strong>, which is the familiar
      continuous-gradient intuition. Near the grid's highest frequencies,
      however, the discrete expression is the accurate one.
    </p>

    <h2>The subtlety: the loss is local, but the FFT is global</h2>
    <p>
      Let <strong>T₀</strong> be the initial estimate and
      <strong>R₀ = I − T₀</strong> its residual. For each pixel, the prototype
      forms a gradient inner product, summing horizontal and vertical
      products (and color channels), then squares that scalar and sums over
      pixels:
    </p>
    <Equation
      expression={String.raw`q_p = \sum_c \left[D_xT_0(p,c)D_xR_0(p,c) + D_yT_0(p,c)D_yR_0(p,c)\right], \qquad L_{\mathrm{excl}} \propto \sum_p q_p^2`}
      label="At each pixel q is the sum of transmission and residual gradient products across axes and channels; the exclusion loss sums the squared q values"
    />
    <p class="math-translation">
      <strong>Translating the math:</strong> At each location, the code measures
      the signed alignment between transmission and residual gradients, then
      penalizes its square. Strong alignment in either direction contributes;
      the penalty is local before it is aggregated.
    </p>
    <p>
      Here is where the apparently straightforward Fourier interpretation becomes
      interesting. The gradient operator is linear. The exclusion loss built from
      its outputs is not. The prototype first computes a local alignment at each
      pixel, then squares that quantity before aggregating it. The order of
      operations matters: squaring a local quantity is not equivalent to
      comparing the global frequency energies of the two images.
    </p>
    <p>
      The FFT gives a clean spectral interpretation of the gradient operator. It
      does not license replacing the complete loss with a frequency-by-frequency
      comparison. Multiplication in image space becomes convolution in frequency
      space, so pointwise products mix frequency components. The loss is therefore
      not just a per-frequency comparison between transmission and reflection.
    </p>

    <h2>Knowing the frequencies isn't the same as knowing where to refine</h2>
    <p>
      The current router scores each tile using the mean absolute spatial
      gradient overlap in the initial estimate and residual. That has a direct
      local interpretation: these are the places where the current estimate
      exhibits the pattern that the refinement prior penalizes. The router then
      spends its finite update budget on the highest-scoring tiles.
    </p>
    <p>
      A whole-image FFT answers a different question. It describes how much
      variation exists at different spatial scales across the image, but it does
      not say <em>where</em> a particular frequency occurs. A sharp edge in one
      corner and the same edge elsewhere can produce similar global spectra. That
      makes a global spectrum a poor direct replacement for a spatial tile score.
    </p>
    <p>
      We could compute local FFTs tile by tile, or use a windowed transform such
      as a short-time Fourier transform. Those choices recover some location
      information, but introduce new decisions: window size, overlap, boundary
      handling, and which frequency bands to score. They also add routing work.
      Since the experiment's premise is a limited refinement budget, that overhead
      must be measured rather than assumed to be free.
    </p>

    <h2>How would we know if the FFT helps?</h2>
    <p>
      The FFT is most useful here as a hypothesis generator. We could ask whether
      local frequency features predict which tile benefits most from one
      refinement update. To test that, keep the starting estimate, candidate
      tiles, update rule, and number of selected tiles fixed. Then compare:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>the existing spatial gradient-overlap score;</li>
      <li>a local FFT score, defined in advance from selected frequency bands;</li>
      <li>uniform allocation, plus random routing as a simple control.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      For a rigorous comparison, distinguish the <strong>actual refinement
      benefit</strong> from the exclusion score itself. For candidate tile
      <strong>i</strong>, define an oracle improvement:
    </p>
    <Equation
      expression={String.raw`\Delta_i = \mathcal{E}(T_0) - \mathcal{E}(T_0^{(i)})`}
      label="Oracle improvement for tile i is the reduction in reconstruction error after applying the candidate refinement update"
    />
    <p class="math-translation">
      <strong>Translating the math:</strong> A positive <strong>Δ<sub>i</sub></strong>
      means the update improved reconstruction quality. The router is not trying to
      identify interesting frequencies in the abstract; it is trying to prioritize
      updates that produce real benefit under a fixed budget.
    </p>
    <p>
      Evaluate the same synthetic scenes and budgets, report reconstruction quality
      against the known clean layer, and include both refinement time and end-to-end
      time with routing overhead. A ranking metric such as Spearman correlation
      between candidate scores and measured <strong>Δ<sub>i</sub></strong> values
      can be informative. The key question is whether the FFT-based strategy
      predicts useful refinement better than the existing score, and whether the
      improvement justifies the extra computation.
    </p>

    <h2>What the Fourier view actually buys us</h2>
    <p>
      The FFT does not reveal a frequency-domain router hiding inside the
      existing prototype. What it reveals is a more precise interpretation of
      the gradient-exclusion prior. Finite differences weight spatial variation
      according to frequency, while the pointwise exclusion loss couples these
      variations through local products. The resulting objective is more
      complicated than a simple comparison of transmission and residual energy at
      each frequency.
    </p>
    <p>
      That distinction matters because the router is not trying to characterize
      an image for its own sake. It is trying to decide where a limited amount of
      computation will be most useful. A global spectrum can tell us which scales
      are present. Local spectral features may tell us more about where those
      scales occur. But neither automatically tells us which refinement update
      will improve the reconstruction.
    </p>
    <p>
      That last question is empirical. The next step is not to replace the
      spatial router with an FFT. It is to test whether local frequency
      information predicts refinement benefit better than the existing score and
      whether the gain is worth its computational cost.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>The interesting connection is not that gradients have a Fourier
      representation. It is that understanding the representation may help us ask a
      better question about where computation should go.</strong>
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
